## What Could Go Wrong

### 1. `[ngValue]` vs `[value]` type mismatch
If `[value]` is used instead of `[ngValue]`, `pageSize` (number) won't match
the option values (strings) and the dropdown will always show blank. Fixed by
using `[ngValue]` as shown above.

### 2. Removing `pageSize` from callers
Three templates previously passed `[pageSize]` to `app-show-candidates` and are
cleaned up as part of this ticket:

| File | Change |
|------|--------|
| `candidates-list.component.html` | Remove `[pageSize]="pageSize"` |
| `candidates-list.component.ts` | Remove `pageSize` field and URL-param read — it was only used to feed show-candidates |
| `define-search.component.html` | Remove `[pageSize]="pageSize"` from `app-show-candidates` only — define-search's own `@Input() pageSize` and its use at line 419 (`request.pageSize = this.pageSize`) are unrelated to show-candidates and must stay |
| `view-job-submission-list.component.html` | Remove `[pageSize]="20"` |

`show-candidates.component.ts`: remove `@Input() declare pageSize: number` —
`pageSize` becomes a plain instance variable initialized solely by
`loadPageSizePreference()`.

### 3. Corrupt / out-of-range localStorage value
The `includes(parsed)` guard in `loadPageSizePreference()` handles this: any
value not in the current options set (including `NaN`, old values like 12 or 20,
stale options) falls through to the type-specific default — 10 for SavedList,
25 for SavedSearch.

### 4. Page number out of bounds after size change
When the user enlarges page size (e.g. from 12 to 50), they may have been on
page 4. Resetting `pageNumber = 1` in `onPageSizeChange` prevents the server
from receiving a page-number that no longer exists.

### 5. Cache invalidation
After a page-size change, `doSearch(true)` is called with `refresh=true`.
`checkCache` skips cache reads when `refresh=true`, so the server is always
hit. The cache is then repopulated via `cacheResults()` with the new page size.
No explicit cache invalidation is needed.

